<http://data.silknow.org/object/431b4bd8-8663-3b3d-84cd-1b8393077d27>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This is a typical woven ribbon of the mid-19th century, which would have been used to decorate women's garments and bonnets. The ribbon was exhibited in the section devoted to Silk (in the sub-section on Fancy Ribbons) at the 1851 Great Exhibition and was also illustrated in the <i>Art Journal</i>catalogue of the exhibition.  \n\nIt was designed by M. Clack, a pupil of the Coventry School of Design and depicts a richly coloured and naturistically realised band of woven floral design on a cream ground. Coventry was famous for manufacturing highly sophisticated ribbons of Jacquard-woven silk. Introduced into Britain from France at the beginning of the  century, the jacquard loom was not widely adopted for industrial use until the 1830s. It allowed a far greater range of patterns to be woven than had previously been possible"@en , "Ribbon, Jacquard-woven, silk, by M.Clack, Coventry, 1850"@en , "British Galleries:\nThe Great Exhibition was intended as a forum for displaying developments in technology. The textile industry was revolutionised by numerous technical inventions in the 19th century. These complex designs, woven on a jacquard loom, were so successful that such ribbons came to be known as Coventry 'Town Ribbons'. [27/03/2003]"@en , "Jacquard-woven silk ribbon, multi coloured naturalistic flowers on  a cream ground"@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/f1ba2b3d-368a-3a5e-9dcc-60b6dfca6dc0> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/98eff1c5-fadb-3377-8f72-cadbcb0cd6e0> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Jacquard-woven silk ribbon, multi coloured naturalistic flowers on  a cream ground"@en , "British Galleries:\nThe Great Exhibition was intended as a forum for displaying developments in technology. The textile industry was revolutionised by numerous technical inventions in the 19th century. These complex designs, woven on a jacquard loom, were so successful that such ribbons came to be known as Coventry 'Town Ribbons'. [27/03/2003]"@en , "This is a typical woven ribbon of the mid-19th century, which would have been used to decorate women's garments and bonnets. The ribbon was exhibited in the section devoted to Silk (in the sub-section on Fancy Ribbons) at the 1851 Great Exhibition and was also illustrated in the <i>Art Journal</i>catalogue of the exhibition.  \n\nIt was designed by M. Clack, a pupil of the Coventry School of Design and depicts a richly coloured and naturistically realised band of woven floral design on a cream ground. Coventry was famous for manufacturing highly sophisticated ribbons of Jacquard-woven silk. Introduced into Britain from France at the beginning of the  century, the jacquard loom was not widely adopted for industrial use until the 1830s. It allowed a far greater range of patterns to be woven than had previously been possible"@en , "Ribbon, Jacquard-woven, silk, by M.Clack, Coventry, 1850"@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/431b4bd8-8663-3b3d-84cd-1b8393077d27/dimension/2> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/431b4bd8-8663-3b3d-84cd-1b8393077d27/dimension/1> ;
        dc:identifier                  "AP.394:4" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O224779> .

